# Environment variables

Copy this file to `.env.local` for local development:

```bash
cp .env.example .env.local
```

`.env*` is git-ignored, so `.env.local` is never committed. On Vercel, set the
same variables in **Project → Settings → Environment Variables** instead.

---

## Required

### `AUTH_SECRET`
Secret used to sign the admin session cookie (HMAC-SHA256).

Generate one:

```bash
node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64'))"
```

Changing it invalidates every active admin session, so all admins must sign in
again. Rotating it is how you force-log-out a compromised session.

### `ADMIN_PASSWORD`
The dashboard password at `/admin/login`.

Generate one:

```bash
node -e "console.log('Thala-' + require('crypto').randomBytes(15).toString('base64url'))"
```

If this is unset the app falls back to the development password `thala2026` and
the admin UI shows a warning. That fallback works in production too, which is why
the check is explicit — never deploy without setting this variable.

Two things to know before changing it:

- The value is compared verbatim, so a password with a space (`thala source`) is
  valid but is easy to mistype on a phone keyboard. Set it in the dashboard's
  Environment Variables editor rather than through `vercel env add`, which reads
  from stdin and can trim trailing whitespace.
- The password is not hashed anywhere; it is compared in constant time against
  `process.env.ADMIN_PASSWORD` in `lib/auth.ts`. Rotating it is therefore a single
  env change and needs no migration.

> **Strength warning.** The live shop currently runs a password chosen from the
> brand name. It is short, contains a space that invites typos, and is among the
> first things anyone would try. The login route throttles to 8 attempts per 10
> minutes per IP, but that throttle is per-instance on Vercel and resets whenever
> a different lambda warms up, so it is not a reliable second line. If this is a
> real shop, replace it with a generated passphrase before handing it over.

---

## Optional — Supabase (shared database)

Without these variables the shop runs in **local mode**: the catalogue,
tariffs and orders live in the browser's `localStorage`. That is fine for
previewing the storefront, but every visitor sees their own copy of the shop and
admin changes are not visible to customers.

Set all three to enable the shared database:

| Variable | Where to get it |
| --- | --- |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL` | Supabase → Project Settings → API |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` | Supabase → Project Settings → API |
| `ADMIN_TOKEN` | Supabase → Project Settings → API → **service_role** key |

`ADMIN_TOKEN` is required for anything that writes or reads orders. Without it the
storefront still works — it can read the catalogue with the public pair — and the
admin screens answer with an explicit message naming the missing variable.

### Product photo uploads

The admin product form uploads photos straight to Supabase Storage (bucket
`product-images`) — there is no filesystem fallback, because Vercel's server
filesystem is read-only. `POST /api/upload` therefore requires all three
variables above and returns a clear `503` listing them when they are missing.

Photos are compressed **in the browser** before they are sent (WebP, longest edge
1600px) and then decoded, rotated, resized and re-encoded again by `lib/images.ts`.
The first step is not redundant: Vercel rejects request bodies over ~4.5 MB before
a Route Handler runs, so an uncompressed 8 MB phone photo would fail in transit.
The server remains the authority on what is actually stored.

The object path is `products/<uuid>.webp`; the admin's filename is discarded
entirely, so there is no collision risk and no path-traversal surface. Deleting a
photo is refused while any product still references it, so the two seed dresses
that share a cover cannot break each other.

### Moving the old catalogue across

The previous catalogue lived only in `localStorage`, so switching backends starts
from an empty database. Two ways to fill it, neither of which involves re-typing:

- run `supabase/schema.sql`, which seeds the four dresses (`on conflict do
  nothing`, so re-running never clobbers your edits);
- or press **"Importer le catalogue local"** in `/admin/products`, which posts the
  products from that browser's `localStorage`. Existing ids are skipped rather than
  overwritten, so pressing it twice, or from several browsers, is harmless.

### Security notes

- `ADMIN_TOKEN` is the **service_role** key. It bypasses Row Level Security, so it
  is only ever used inside Route Handlers. It is never prefixed with
  `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, which is exactly what stops Next.js from inlining it into the
  client bundle. Do not rename it to anything `NEXT_PUBLIC_`-prefixed —
  `lib/db/supabase.ts` has `import "server-only"` so that mistake fails the build
  instead of shipping the key.
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` values are inlined into the browser bundle and are therefore
  public. The anon key is designed for this; never use the service_role key there.
- RLS grants the anon key **read-only** access to `products` and `shipping_rates`.
  It has no insert, update or delete policy anywhere, including on `orders`: a
  visitor places an order through `POST /api/orders`, which recomputes every price
  server-side before inserting.

Apply `supabase/schema.sql` in the Supabase SQL editor before the first run. It
creates the `products`, `shipping_rates` and `orders` tables, seeds the
catalogue, creates the public-read `product-images` Storage bucket, and enables
Row Level Security. It is safe to re-run — re-running also *tightens* an
installation created from an older, more permissive version of the file.